## Motivation

An action to scroll to an element was not available through `UIAutomator`.  As with Jetpack Compose Views we have to rely on `UIAutomator` to interact with the elements on the screen, it was impossible for us to scroll to elements in such screens. 

## Proposed solution

Add two new commands to be able to scroll from `UIAutomator`:
* `uiautomator_horizontally_scroll_to_element`
* `uiautomator_vertically_scroll_to_element`


The commands proposed syntax is very similar to the one used to execute actions, we want the command to be as flexible as possible so the developer can decide how to find the element and also the container where to scroll:

 `perform_action('uiautomator_vertically_scroll_to_element', 'targetViewStrategy', 'targetViewLocator', 'containerViewStrategy', 'containerViewLocator', maxScrollAttempts)` 

 `perform_action('uiautomator_horizontally_scroll_to_element', 'targetViewStrategy', 'targetViewLocator', 'containerViewStrategy', 'containerViewLocator', maxScrollAttempts)` 

Example of use:

* We can scroll vertically to an element using its text without providing any container strategy or locator:

`perform_action('uiautomator_vertically_scroll_to_element', 'textContains', 'Rice')`

* We can scroll horizontally to an element targeting its text and finding the container to scroll by its content description:

`perform_action('uiautomator_horizontally_scroll_to_element','text','Image 6', 'desc', 'SampleImagesContainer')`

Note: these actions, would also work for non-compose views. Example of use:
* If we want to find a view with a specific id scrolling through a view that is a `LinearLayout` we could do the following:

`perform_action('uiautomator_vertically_scroll_to_element', 'res', 'com.xing.android:id/carouselView', 'clazz','android.widget.LinearLayout')`

* And then horizontally scroll until a text identifying the scrollable container with its id.

## Problems not solved

Sometimes, when scrolling to an element, when the scrolling is over, the element is not fully visible. The reason for this is that the method we use to scroll (`UiScrollableCustom.scrollIntoView`) is only scrolling until the element “exists” so it could be that only a small part of it is actually visible on the screen. 

There is a method `UiScrollable.ensureFullyVisible` that we tried to use to scroll until the view is fully visible. This method basically checks if the _view bounds_ of the element are the same as the _visible view bounds_ of the element. The problem is that the view bounds and the visible view bounds are always the same so this method always considers the view as fully visible.

This is the method we used to test the view bounds values:

```java
    public void scrollUntilFullyVisible(UiSelector selector) throws UiObjectNotFoundException {
        UiObject targetObject = this.getChild(selector);
        Rect actual = targetObject.getBounds();
        Log.i("Test","Actual bounds: "+actual);
        boolean isFullyVisible = false;
        while (!isFullyVisible) {
            Rect visible = targetObject.getVisibleBounds();
            Log.i("Test","Visible bounds: "+visible);
            isFullyVisible = visible.width() * visible.height() == actual.width() * actual.height();
            if (!isFullyVisible) {
                this.ensureFullyVisible(targetObject);
            }
        }
    }
 ```
 
 We added it to `UiScrollableCustom` and called it from `scrollIntoView` as follows:

```java
{...}
 for (int x = 0; x < getMaxSearchSwipes() && !found; ++x) {
            if (this.exists(childSelector)) {
                scrollUntilFullyVisible(selector);
                found = true;
            }
{...}
```

This problem should be analyzed and solved somehow but it is out of the scope of this PR.

